% VERIFIED (READ) -- Tables 1, 2 and 3 read from the accepted manuscript on
% 2026-09-22. THIS IS THE REFERENCE THE REPORT NEEDS MOST.
%
% Page range: the Chalmers cover page gives vol. 39, pp. 347--370 with DOI
% 10.1016/j.jfluidstructs.2013.02.007. Some indexes list 500--511; trust the
% cover page but confirm once on ScienceDirect.
%
% WHY IT MATTERS -- three things, all read from the paper:
%
% 1. Their Table 1 compiles EIGHT independent studies at Re = 100 with
%    comparable large domains. Cd values: Park et al. (1998) 1.33,
%    Kravchenko and Moin (1998) 1.32, Shi et al. (2004) 1.318,
%    Mittal (2005) 1.322, Stalberg et al. (2006) 1.32, Posdziech and
%    Grundmann (2007) 1.325, Li et al. (2009) 1.336, present 1.319.
%    St values cluster at 0.164--0.166 and Cl' (r.m.s. lift) at 0.222--0.235.
%    So the Re = 100 consensus is Cd ~ 1.32, NOT the "1.37--1.38" the report
%    asserts without a citation. The report's Cd validation claim of +0.3 %
%    is really about +4.8 %. The St benchmark of 0.165 and the Cl' benchmark
%    of ~0.23 are both correct and now citable from here.
%
% 2. Their Table 3 gives Cd, Cl', -Cpb, Lc, separation angle and St at
%    Re = 50, 60, 80, 100, 120, 150, 180, 200 at large domains (H = 100-200):
%      Re    H     Cd      Cl'     -Cpb    Lc    theta_s   St
%      50   200   1.397   0.0399   0.511   2.46   124.0   0.1240
%      60   160   1.377   0.0871   0.560   2.11   123.0   0.1354
%      80   160   1.336   0.1616   0.640   1.67   120.3   0.1528
%     100   120   1.317   0.2224   0.707   1.42   118.0   0.1649
%     120   120   1.306   0.2776   0.764   1.25   116.4   0.1739
%     150   100   1.305   0.3546   0.846   1.06   114.7   0.1841
%     150   160   1.301   0.3529   0.840   1.06   114.7   0.1837
%     180   100   1.310   0.4251   0.921   0.93   113.5   0.1919
%     200   100   1.316   0.4678   0.966   0.86   112.9   0.1958
%    That covers EVERY Reynolds number in this project's sweep. Use it to
%    validate the whole sweep, not just Re = 100.
%
% 3. Confinement is their explicit subject and their lateral condition
%    (u = 1, v = 0, called the free stream condition) is this project's
%    free-slip setup. They report, via Kumar and Mittal (2006), that blockage
%    effects are negligible only for H > 100, and via Posdziech and Grundmann
%    (2007) that "the force coefficient and the base suction coefficient are
%    strongly dependent on the resolution and even more on the size of the
%    computational domain" while St is not. This project's H = 50 (2 %
%    blockage) is below that threshold and below every domain in their tables,
%    which explains a systematic Cd overshoot and supports the report's
%    headline blockage finding with a citation.
@article{qu2013,
  author  = {Qu, Lixia and Norberg, Christoffer and Davidson, Lars and
             Peng, Shia-Hui and Wang, Fujun},
  title   = {Quantitative numerical analysis of flow past a circular cylinder
             at Reynolds number between 50 and 200},
  journal = {Journal of Fluids and Structures},
  volume  = {39},
  pages   = {347--370},
  year    = {2013},
  doi     = {10.1016/j.jfluidstructs.2013.02.007}
}

